"Don’t Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1)\nciteturn41258view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1\",\"lineno\":168}); Total lines: 600\nL157:  | $$\\{\\Delta W_{u,\\ell}\\}_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}=F_{\\phi}(E_{u}),\\qquad W_{u,\\ell}=W_{\\theta,\\ell}+\\Delta W_{u,\\ell}.$$  |  | (2)\nL158: The updates $\\Delta\\theta_{u}=(\\Delta W_{u,\\ell})_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ produce the adapted policy $\\pi_{\\theta+\\Delta\\theta_{u}}$, with all other parameters unchanged. The objective is to maximize task success on $u$ without target demonstrations or parameter optimization on the target interface.\nL159: $F_{\\phi}$ has three parts, described below: a frozen bank of adapters, a small router with the trainable parameters $\\phi$, and a frozen scout that maps the record $E_{v}$ of an interface $v$ to a representation $h_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}$.\nL160: ##### The adapter bank.\nL161: The bank holds one LoRA adapter per source interface. For each $v_{k}$, the bank contains factors $\\{(A^{(k)}_{\\ell},B^{(k)}_{\\ell})\\}_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ of rank $r$ and scale $\\alpha$, with updates $\\Delta W^{(k)}_{\\ell}=(\\alpha/r)\\,B^{(k)}_{\\ell}A^{(k)}_{\\ell}$. We write $\\Delta\\theta^{(k)}=(\\Delta W^{(k)}_{\\ell})_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ for adapter $k$, and $\\pi^{(k)}=\\pi_{\\theta+\\Delta\\theta^{(k)}}$ for the policy using it.\nL162: The bank $\\{\\Delta\\theta^{(1)},\\dots,\\Delta\\theta^{(K)}\\}$ is the source knowledge that WebMix transfers to an unseen interface. All bank adapters share the base model, the index set $\\Omega$, the rank $r$, and the scale $\\alpha$, so they can be combined matrix-wise. The bank is trained once (Stage 1, §cite22†3.3.1 ) and stays frozen afterwards.\nL163: ##### What the hypernetwork predicts.\nL164: \nL165: A direct hypernetwork, such as Text-to-LoRA (cite85†Charakorn et al., 2025 ), predicts every entry of every LoRA factor. The router of WebMix predicts only $K$ mixing coefficients, one per bank adapter. It is a two-layer perceptron with parameters $\\phi=(U_{1},b_{1},U_{2},b_{2})$ that maps $h_{v}$ to a vector of mixture weights over the $K$ bank adapters,\nL166:  | $$c_{v}=\\operatorname{softmax}\\!\\left(U_{2}\\,\\operatorname{SiLU}(U_{1}h_{v}+b_{1})+b_{2}\\right)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K},$$  |  | (3)\nL167: where $U_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\\times d_{c}}$, $b_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}}$, $U_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K\\times d_{h}}$, $b_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K}$, and $d_{h}$ is the hidden width ($d_{h}=256$, Appendix cite39†A.3 ). The coefficient $c_{v,k}$ is the weight of bank adapter $k$ in the adapter for $v$. A single coefficient vector is shared across all adapted matrices. We initialize $U_{2}$ and $b_{2}$ to zero, so the untrained router weights all bank adapters equally.\nL168: ##### How the bank is combined.\nL169: \nL170: The adapter for interface $v$ is the weighted sum of the bank’s updates,\nL171: \nL172:  | $$\\Delta W_{v,\\ell}=\\sum_{k=1}^{K}c_{v,k}\\,\\Delta W^{(k)}_{\\ell}.$$  |  | (4)\nL173: \nL174: We compute this sum by concatenating the bank factors along their rank dimension,\nL175: \nL176:  | $$A_{v,\\ell}=\\begin{bmatrix}c_{v,1}A^{(1)}_{\\ell}\\\\\nL177: \\vdots\\\\\nL178: c_{v,K}A^{(K)}_{\\ell}\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad B_{v,\\ell}=\\begin{bmatrix}B^{(1)}_{\\ell}&\\cdots&B^{(K)}_{\\ell}\\end{bmatrix}.$$  |  | (5)\nL179: The product satisfies $B_{v,\\ell}A_{v,\\ell}=\\sum_{k}c_{v,k}B^{(k)}_{\\ell}A^{(k)}_{\\ell}$. Averaging the two factors separately would instead weight each adapter’s own term by $c_{v,k}^{2}$ and add cross terms $B^{(j)}_{\\ell}A^{(k)}_{\\ell}$ with $j\\neq k$, which by Eq. cite116†9 (Appendix cite37†A.1 ) depends on how each adapter happens to be factored.\nL180: The composed factors have inner dimension $r_{\\mathrm{out}}=Kr$, and we set $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{out}}=K\\alpha$, so that $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{out}}/r_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\alpha/r$. The result is a standard LoRA adapter of rank at most $Kr$ and scale $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{out}}$, and installing it through Eq. cite117†2 adds the same update as Eq. cite118†4 to the base weights. It is served by an unmodified LoRA inference stack.\nL181: ##### The scout.\nL182: The scout turns a bounded visit to an interface $v$ into the representation $h_{v}$ that the router reads. It has three parts: an explorer collects an exploration record $E_{v}$, a writer summarizes $E_{v}$ into a text interface manual $m_{v}$, and a frozen sentence encoder $\\psi$ embeds $m_{v}$. None of the three is trained at any stage. Appendix cite41†A.5 describes each part. The scout returns two objects for an interface $v$. The first is the manual $m_{v}$, a readable text summary of the interface.\nL183: The second is the conditioning vector, the normalized encoding of the manual,\nL184:  | $$h_{v}=\\frac{\\psi(m_{v})}{\\|\\psi(m_{v})\\|_{2}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}.$$  |  | (6)\nL185: \nL186: Only $h_{v}$ enters the hypernetwork, where the router maps it to the mixing coefficients $c_{v}$ of Eq. cite119†3 .\nL187: ### 3.3 Training and Adaptation\nL188: \nL189: WebMix assembles the parameters from low-rank adapters. It trains one adapter per source interface, keeps the adapters in a frozen bank, and learns a small network, the router, that decides how much of each adapter to use on a given interface. Learning and adaptation proceed in three stages:\nL190: \nL191:   1. 1.\nL192: \nL193: Stage 1: Adapter Bank Training (§cite22†3.3.1 ). For each source interface, we fine-tune one adapter on its demonstrations and freeze it in the bank.\nL194: \nL195:   2. 2.\nL196: Stage 2: Router Training (§cite23†3.3.2 ). The router learns to map the scout’s encoded manual of each source interface to mixing weights over the bank, by imitating its demonstrations through the frozen base model and bank.\nL197: \nL198:   3. 3.\nL199: \nL200: Stage 3: Adaptation (§cite24†3.3.3 ). The scout writes a manual of the unseen interface, and the router mixes the bank into the agent’s adapter for it in one forward pass.\nL201: Both training stages use only source-interface data, so the scouting visit is the only information from the unseen interface that WebMix uses. Adding a source interface later adds one adapter to the bank and retrains the router, leaving the existing adapters unchanged.\nL202: #### 3.3.1 Stage 1: Adapter Bank Training\nL203: Each adapter $k$ is trained with supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to reproduce the expert on its own interface. An anchoring term also limits how far the adapter moves the base policy on the other source interfaces. We call $\\mathcal{D}_{k}$ the adapter’s own corpus, and $\\mathcal{D}_{\\setminus k}=\\bigcup_{j\\neq k}\\mathcal{D}_{j}$ its anchor corpus, which pools the demonstrations of the other $K-1$ source interfaces. Neither corpus contains data from an unseen interface. We minimize the objective\nL204:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{bank}}^{(k)}=\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{k}}\\left[-\\frac{1}{|y|}\\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\\log\\pi^{(k)}(y_{i}\\mid x,y_{<i})\\right]}_{\\text{SFT on training interface}}+\\beta\\,\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{\\setminus k}}\\left[\\frac{1}{|y|}\\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\\kappa^{(k)}_{i}\\right]}_{\\text{anchor on other source interfaces}},$$  |  | (7)\nL205: where $\\beta\\geq 0$ controls the anchoring strength and $\\beta=0$ recovers plain SFT. The first term is the SFT loss on the adapter’s own interface. The second term penalizes changes to the base policy’s likelihood of the demonstrated tokens of the other source interfaces, where $\\kappa^{(k)}_{i}$ is a clipped estimate of the KL divergence (Appendix cite38†A.2 ).\nL206: #### 3.3.2 Stage 2: Training the Router\nL207: Stage 2 trains the router with the bank frozen. Before training, the scout visits each source interface $v_{k}$ once and returns its representation $h_{v_{k}}$. From $h_{v_{k}}$, Eqs. cite119†3 and cite118†4 construct the composed update $\\Delta\\theta_{\\phi}(h_{v_{k}})=(\\Delta W_{v_{k},\\ell})_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$. Let $\\pi_{\\phi,k}=\\pi_{\\theta+\\Delta\\theta_{\\phi}(h_{v_{k}})}$ denote the resulting policy.\nL208: We train the router with the behavior-cloning (BC) loss averaged over all response tokens of the pooled source corpus,\nL209:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{router}}(\\phi)=-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\sum_{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{k}}\\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\\log\\pi_{\\phi,k}(y_{i}\\mid x,y_{<i}),\\qquad N=\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\sum_{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{k}}|y|.$$  |  | (8)\nL210: Prompt tokens are masked, so only the expert’s response tokens contribute. Gradients reach the router through the composed updates and the frozen base model, so the router learns which mixtures reproduce the expert on each source interface. Only $\\phi$ is trained. The base model, the bank, and the scout stay frozen, and the representations $h_{v_{k}}$ are fixed inputs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructured Authority Injection: Persistent Human Control for LLM Agents via Typed MCP Overrides (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1)\nciteturn41258view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1\",\"lineno\":190}); Total lines: 298\nL163: TABLE I: Scenario details: eight planning failure modes.\nL164: #  | Failure mode  | Setup summary  | SAI class\nL165: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL166: S1  | Priority mis-order  | 2 robots; high-priority medical task far from spawn  | Priority\nL167: S2  | Zone distribution  | 3 robots clustered left; must spread across both zones  | Strategy\nL168: S3  | Compound  | 3 robots; satisfy priority + zone rules simultaneously  | P + S\nL169: S4  | Late interrupt  | SearchTask spawns at tick 10; redirect robot  | Priority\nL170: S5  | Unreachable task  | High-priority task in sealed room; must not assign  | Constraint\nL171: S6  | Capability mismatch  | 6 robots $\\times$ 3 types; 8 tasks with requirements  | Constraint\nL172: S7  | Bottleneck  | 6 robots; single-cell corridor; limit concurrency  | Strategy\nL173: S8  | Formation  | 5 robots; 2 cargo tasks need $\\geq$3 robots; sequence  | Strategy\nL174: ## IV Experimental Evaluation\nL175: \nL176: TABLE II: Task success rate comparison between baseline and SAI across eight scenarios.\nL177:  | S1  | S2  | S3  | S4  | S5  | S6  | S7  | S8\nL178: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL179: LLM  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI\nL180: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL181: ASI1  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 0.00  | 0.00  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 0.88  | 1.00  | 0.86  | 1.00  | 0.00\nL182: Claude-Haiku  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.83  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.50\nL183: DeepSeek-R1  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.00  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | –  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL184: Gemini-2.0-Flash  | 0.50  | 0.50  | 1.00  | 0.50  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 0.88  | 0.43  | 0.57  | 1.00  | 0.50\nL185: Gemma-4-26B  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 0.88  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL186: GPT-4o  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 0.83  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 0.88  | 0.88  | 1.00  | 0.57  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL187: GPT-OSS-20B  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.00  | 1.00  | 0.20  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 0.17  | 0.00  | 0.75  | 0.63  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.57  | 0.00  | 1.00\nL188: LLaMA-3.3-70B  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.00  | 0.50  | 0.88  | 0.88  | 0.43  | 0.00  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL189: Qwen3-32B  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 0.88  | 0.88  | 0.43  | 0.71  | 0.00  | 1.00\nL190: Note: Green cells indicate improvement and red cells indicate degradation. Bold values denote the better result between conditions. A dash (–) denotes an excluded run: DeepSeek-R1 under SAI in S7 exhausted its 4,096-token generation budget before producing any assignment.\nL191: \nL192: cite57†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Baseline failure vs. SAI under an obstructed task. Robots are colored in blue, and targets are colored in green.\nL193: ### IV-A Scenario and Setup\nL194: \nL195: We design eight scenarios each targeting one distinct failure mode (Table cite58†I ). The Baseline condition gives the LLM no pre-prompting beyond the world state. The SAI condition injects relevant context as a typed SAI override before the first planning epoch. This isolates each failure mode so that performance differences are attributable to the override mechanism, not task difficulty.\nL196: Nine LLMs are evaluated: GPT-4o [cite59†26 ], DeepSeek-R1 [cite60†12 ], Qwen3-32B [cite61†35 ], Claude-Haiku [cite62†2 ], Gemini-2.0-Flash [cite63†7 ], LLaMA-3.3-70B [cite64†11 ], Gemma-4-26B [cite65†33 ], GPT-OSS-20B [cite66†27 ], and ASI1 [cite67†30 ]. Each model $\\times$ condition pair is run five times on 100-tick simulations.\nL197: All eight scenarios are instantiated in a MuJoCo-based grid-world simulator, where each robot is modeled as a kinematic agent with discrete move and interact actions, and task completion is detected automatically via proximity and state-change triggers.\nL198: ### IV-B Override Correctness\nL199: Table cite68†II reports task success rates across all models and scenarios. SAI recovers completions for models with partial failures, for instance, Claude-Haiku improves from 0.75 to 1.00 in S1, and GPT-OSS-20B recovers from 0.00 to 1.00 in both S2 and S3, but cannot repair systematic omission: Gemini-2.0-Flash remains at 0.50 in S1 under both conditions.\nL200: Notably, Gemini-2.0-Flash also regresses from 1.00 to 0.50 in S2 under SAI, confirming that structured injection can disrupt models already performing well.\nL201: In S1–S3, most models operate near ceiling under baseline, leaving little room for SAI to improve. S4 (late interrupt) exposes the sharpest divergence: SAI improves DeepSeek-R1, Gemini, Gemma-4, and Claude-Haiku (each $+$0.17–$+$0.20), while severely degrading ASI1 (0.80$\\to$0.00) and GPT-OSS (0.80$\\to$0.17), indicating that sensitivity to structured context injection is model-dependent rather than task-difficulty-dependent.\nL202: The constraint-heavy scenarios (S5–S8) amplify these heterogeneous effects. SAI yields large gains in S5 for ASI1, GPT-OSS, and DeepSeek-R1 (all 0.00$\\to$0.75), but leaves models already at 0.75 baseline unchanged. In S8, GPT-OSS and Qwen3 both recover from 0.00 to 1.00, while ASI1 collapses in the same scenario (1.00$\\to$0.00), and GPT-4o and LLaMA drop sharply in S7 ($-$0.43 each).\nL203: DeepSeek-R1’s S7 result under SAI is excluded: its single planning call exhausted the 4,096-token output limit before producing any assignments, as the combined authority context and seven-task state inflated the reasoning chain beyond the model’s generation budget. Overall, SAI acts as a high-variance intervention, which is beneficial in under-constrained or previously failed settings, but destabilising in tightly coupled multi-constraint regimes.\nL204: Case Study: Persistent Task Misallocation. Figure cite69†2 shows a case study for 3 ant robots in scenario 5. At tick 0, both Baseline and SAI begin from identical robot configurations. By tick 8, the Baseline planner has already collapsed into a persistent invalid allocation: the upper-left robot remains assigned to an obstructed and unreachable task, while the remaining robots continue normal navigation.\nL205: In contrast, SAI redirects all robots away from the blocked objective through structured authority constraints, producing a globally feasible allocation. By tick 20, SAI successfully completes the scenario, whereas the Baseline system remains deadlocked with the same robot still targeting the impossible task. This case illustrates how persistent authority context prevents long-horizon assignment failures that the Baseline planner cannot recover from.\nL206: ### IV-C Authority Persistence\nL207: \nL208: cite70†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Robot time-state distribution across scenarios. cite71†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Robot time-state distribution across LLMs.\nL209: Figure cite72†3 breaks down robot time across four mutually exclusive states averaged over all nine LLMs per scenario-condition pair, including Working (executing a task on-site), Moving (traveling toward an assignment), Stuck (assigned but physically blocked for that tick by a collision or obstacle), and Pure Idle (no current assignment).\nL210: Pure Idle dominates in every scenario ($36$–$87\\%$), a structural consequence of the batched planning loop and post-completion dead ticks; high idle therefore coexists with perfect success rates and does not imply planning failure. The informative cross-condition signals are in Moving and Stuck.\nL211: SAI sharply increases Moving in S4 ($26\\%\\!\\to\\!44\\%$) as the late interrupt forces robots to re-navigate to reprioritised targets, and nearly eliminates Stuck in S3 ($9\\%\\!\\to\\!0\\%$) where structured zone constraints resolve baseline spatial conflicts. S7 carries the highest Stuck burden overall ($\\approx\\!27\\%$ baseline), while S5 shows the broadest activation increase ($13\\%\\!\\to\\!30\\%$), consistent with the completion gains in Table cite68†II .\nL212: Figure cite73†4 applies the same decomposition by LLM across all eight scenarios. Working time is near-uniform ($6$–$10\\%$) across all models, confirming that on-task execution is model-agnostic; planning quality differences surface in Moving and Stuck instead. GPT-OSS-20B records the lowest baseline active time ($\\approx\\!16\\%$), which SAI doubles to $\\approx\\!32\\%$, consistent with its collapsed success rates in S2, S3, and S5.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTrust but Verify: Designing Region-InhomogeneousForward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1)\nciteturn41258view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 288\nL79: The experiments further suggest a regularity, which we offer as an interpretation rather than a tested result: detectability and damage both appear to be governed by the divergence between the corruption kernel and the data conditional. Conceptually, we view absorbing masked diffusion as one end of a trust axis whose continuous analog is the signal-to-noise contrast between known and unknown regions in diffusion inpainting; this framing motivates the method but is not established formally here.\nL80: ## 2 Related work\nL81: #### Discrete diffusion as stochastic dynamics.\nL82: cite42†Austin et al. (2021) introduced discrete diffusion with structured corruption kernels (absorbing, uniform-mixing, and discretized-Gaussian transition matrices), and continuous-time treatments formalize the general setting as Markov jump processes with learnable reverse rates (cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite44†Lou et al., 2024 ).\nL83: Subsequent work specialized to the absorbing kernel, simplifying its variational bound to a weighted masked cross-entropy (cite45†Sahoo et al., 2024 ; cite46†Shi et al., 2024 ) and establishing the time-free property of its optimal denoiser (cite47†Ou et al., 2025 ); LLaDA scaled this recipe to 8B parameters (cite48†Nie et al., 2025 ), and block diffusion interpolates between autoregressive and diffusion decoding with clean context to the left of the active block (cite49†Arriola et al., 2025 ).\nL84: All of these inherit the absorbing process’s binary conditioning interface, which is the object of our study.\nL85: #### Hybrid and self-correcting forward processes.\nL86: Closest to our forward-process design, GIDD generalizes masked diffusion to arbitrary interpolating noise and trains on a spatially homogeneous hybrid of masking and uniform noise, showing the resulting models can identify and correct their own mistakes in generated samples (cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ); SCDD reformulates such pretraining-based self-correction with explicit discrete-time transitions (cite51†Wang et al., 2026 ); and ReMDM adds inference-time re-noising of model-committed tokens to restore iterative refinement (cite52†Wang et al., 2025 ).\nL87: Our setting differs on two axes. The corruption is region-inhomogeneous: hybrid noise is applied only to user-supplied context, targeting trust in the conditioning rather than the model’s own outputs.\nL88: And the asymmetry matters for reliability: GIDD reports that homogeneous hybrid-noise models lag mask-only models downstream, whereas confining the uniform-mixing kernel to the context region, together with an $\\eta{=}0$ mixture, kept clean-conditioning behavior and the held-out variational bound within our pre-registered H3 tolerances at this scale; we did not train a homogeneous hybrid-noise arm, so this contrast is indirect.\nL89: Our arm B further suggests that, in our setting, inference-time re-noising alone does not create the capability: a model trained only under absorbing dynamics assigns no meaningful likelihood to observed positions.\nL90: #### Denoising and editing text.\nL91: SUNDAE trains non-autoregressive generators by unrolled denoising of randomly corrupted sequences (cite53†Savinov et al., 2022 ); DiffusER frames generation as edit sequences (cite54†Reid et al., 2023 ); ELECTRA trains a discriminative replaced-token detector (cite55†Clark et al., 2020 ). We retain the diffusion formulation: detection falls out of the generative model’s own likelihoods rather than a separate discriminator, and repair is performed by the same reverse dynamics that fill holes.\nL92: Our external-detector baseline (Section cite30†4.2 ) gives one point of comparison: a GPT-2 left-context surprisal detector trails the model on conspicuous corruptions and fails on plausible ones. We did not evaluate bidirectional or trained replaced-token detectors such as ELECTRA.\nL93: #### Conditioning in continuous diffusion.\nL94: Gaussian inpainting must reconcile clean observations with reverse-time dynamics that expect globally noised inputs, via renoised composition (cite39†Lugmayr et al., 2022 ) or specialized conditional models; confidence-ordered parallel decoding originates with MaskGIT (cite56†Chang et al., 2022 ); and per-position noise levels as first-class objects appear in Diffusion Forcing (cite40†Chen et al., 2024 ).\nL95: We view our method as a discrete analog on a shared axis: where those approaches lower the known region from full noise to intermediate SNR, we raise it from infinite SNR to finite.\nL96: ## 3 Method\nL97: ### 3.1 Background: absorbing forward dynamics\nL98: Let $x_{0}\\in\\mathcal{V}^{L}$ and let $t\\in[0,1]$ index the absorbing forward process: each token independently jumps to the absorbing state [MASK] so that the forward marginal masks position $i$ with probability $t$. As in standard masked diffusion training, only these marginals enter the objective. The denoiser $p_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid\\tilde{x})$ is a bidirectional Transformer trained with the weighted masked cross-entropy of cite46†Shi et al. (2024) ; cite45†Sahoo et al.\nL99: (2024) , which upper-bounds negative log-likelihood; by the time-free property (cite47†Ou et al., 2025 ), its optimum is $p_{\\mathrm{data}}(x_{0}^{i}\\mid\\tilde{x}^{\\mathrm{UM}})$, the conditional of clean data given the unmasked set, independent of $t$. Conditional infilling fixes a set of positions as clean context and runs the reverse process over the remainder; under absorbing dynamics, observed tokens persist with probability one, and that property, its cost and its repair, is our subject.\nL100: ### 3.2 Region-inhomogeneous forward dynamics\nL101: \nL102: We partition each length-$L{=}512$ sequence into holes $H$ and known context $K$: one to four contiguous spans covering 10–50% of positions form $H$, matching the infilling use case; the rest is $K$. The two regions evolve under different corruption kernels. Draw $t\\sim U(0,1]$ and\nL103: \nL104:  | $$\\eta\\sim\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\delta_{0}+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,U(0,\\eta_{\\max}],\\qquad\\eta_{\\max}=0.15,$$  |  | (1)\nL105: \nL106: and corrupt $x_{0}\\mapsto\\tilde{x}$ position-wise:\nL107:  | $$\\tilde{x}^{i}=\\begin{cases}\\texttt{[MASK]}{}\\text{ w.p. }t,\\;\\;x_{0}^{i}\\text{ otherwise},&i\\in H\\quad\\text{(absorbing kernel)},\\\\[2.0pt]\nL108: z\\sim q_{\\mathrm{noise}}\\text{ w.p. }\\eta,\\;\\;x_{0}^{i}\\text{ otherwise},&i\\in K\\quad\\text{(mixing kernel, }z\\neq x_{0}^{i}\\text{)}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)\nL109: Holes traverse the absorbing dynamics indexed by $t$; the context region is held at a fixed, small elapsed time under a mixing process, its marginal substitution probability $\\eta$ resampled per sequence. The $\\eta{=}0$ component keeps the standard clean-conditioning configuration in-distribution, and 10% of training batches use the plain pretraining objective (no $K/H$ split) to protect unconditional generation.\nL110: We instantiate the mixing kernel $q_{\\mathrm{noise}}$ two ways: (i) uniform over the vocabulary, the classical uniform-mixing kernel of cite42†Austin et al. (2021) ; and (ii) a hard-negative mixture of equal parts uniform draws, corpus-unigram draws, top-32 embedding-space neighbors of the true token, and masked self-proposals from the base model in the spirit of cite55†Clark et al. (2020) . The latter is a data-dependent kernel concentrated nearer the data conditional.\nL111: Evaluation-time plausible corruptions always come from a generator family disjoint from all training noise (Section cite18†4.1 ), so no arm ever sees its test corruption distribution during training.\nL112: ### 3.3 Objective\nL113: \nL114:  | $$\\mathcal{L}(\\theta)\\;=\\;\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\;\\frac{1}{t}\\sum_{i\\in H}\\mathbf{1}\\!\\left[\\tilde{x}^{i}=\\text{M}\\right]\\mathrm{CE}_{i}\\;+\\;\\lambda\\sum_{i\\in K}\\mathrm{CE}_{i}\\;\\right],\\qquad\\mathrm{CE}_{i}=-\\log p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(x_{0}^{i}\\mid\\tilde{x}\\right),$$  |  | (3)\nL115: with both terms normalized per position. The first term is the standard masked loss over holes; the second teaches copy-else-fix at context positions. We use $\\lambda=0.25$ with uniform position weights, set once and not tuned; the defaults worked on the first attempt, and sweeps over $\\lambda$ and substituted-position upweighting are deferred (Section cite32†5 ).\nL116: Equation (cite57†3 ) is a deliberate surrogate rather than the exact variational bound of the composed process: principled ELBOs exist for mixed and interpolating kernels (cite42†Austin et al., 2021 ; cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ), and their per-position weighting for the mixing term differs from ours; since $\\eta$ is small and the target is editor behavior rather than held-out likelihood, the principled weighting is likewise deferred.\nL117: We use no auxiliary input flag marking $K$ as potentially corrupted: content-only detection is the more general capability, and hole geometry is already visible through [MASK].\nL118: ### 3.4 Inference: reverse-time re-noising of the conditioning\nL119: \nL120: Because the finetuned model emits a meaningful distribution at every position, the observed-token probability\nL121: \n"
